topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050705}] :topic/title "Double Integrals Over Type I Regions"}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186730674 :question-generator/id #uuid "e466d5f5-1c83-55c4-9e60-8d4cbc46284f" :question-generator/script "generators/9f/9f8093b29fc615007f9f2c960705985a3d8108aaa8708bd3245fd82055e7c63f/17592186129197.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129643 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9315f9a2-e02a-52a0-9b61-22998fdd4b41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127340} {:answer/id #uuid "a2493839-f867-5e6b-86ab-21e93542af83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127341} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf23205-9937-580f-9487-67df5e047e7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127342} {:answer/id #uuid "fbba71af-ed9d-5b0d-9f28-8c1f36bd3fcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127343} {:answer/id #uuid "176c442b-d740-5c8e-bae2-04e05f97f2e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbba71af-ed9d-5b0d-9f28-8c1f36bd3fcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127343} :answer-field/id #uuid "3868bc82-87b4-5f23-bdbc-a27b10c54df7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127339}] :question/id #uuid "d5cbb250-8e64-5b66-b88a-3ef280b7ad9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-38374" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle u = x - y, v = y,\n$$\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x - y)^{2} - y = 0$ and the line $y = 1$." :question/worked-solution "Under $u=x-y$ and $v=y$, the parabola becomes $v=u^2$ and the line becomes $v=1$. They meet at $u=\\pm1$. The inverse is $x=u+v$, $y=v$, with Jacobian $1$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\int_{-1}^1\\int_{u^2}^1 1\\,dv\\,du\n=\\int_{-1}^1(1-u^2)\\,du=\\frac43.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129878 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fcf7e1f1-fcf9-543c-97ee-b919265bfc35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373306} {:answer/id #uuid "c5bf893e-8435-5b7e-b5c4-19722cff781d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373307} {:answer/id #uuid "4deced36-0d95-50f5-af94-23b1383c8b33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373308} {:answer/id #uuid "3395c3cb-8544-536c-a34a-982908787158" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373309} {:answer/id #uuid "55a5789a-60d9-53af-abe8-31a45948a5e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373310}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5bf893e-8435-5b7e-b5c4-19722cff781d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373307} :answer-field/id #uuid "a50d9972-aaca-5f8a-98d1-1ba9d85277f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373305}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5371e22a-fae1-5a1c-87b6-2b32ed156fc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-82874" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x+y)^{4}-x+y=0$ and  the line $y=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x+y=u$ and $x-y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+y)^{4}-x+y=0\\,⟹\\,(x+y)^{4}-(x-y)=0\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y &=u \\\\ x-y &=v\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{u-v}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into the equation of the given line yields:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=0\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{u-v}{2}=0\\,⟹\\,v=u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &={u}^{4} \\\\ v &=u\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{3}(u-1) &=0 \\\\ v &=u.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the intersection points are $(0,0)$ and $(1,1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,{u}^{4}\\le v\\le u\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/1e/1ed905e319e689d3fc7955a30f58e0f3ae2e7a751c27b80201d2b43f58b4206c.png)\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(-2)^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{u}^{4}}^{u}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[v]_{{u}^{4}}^{u}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(u-{u}^{4})du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{u}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{3}{10}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371906 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33a307a4-5e3d-5e26-9870-e4654bb643c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371908} {:answer/id #uuid "9175a99b-56d0-595b-86db-511244542711" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371909} {:answer/id #uuid "6ae05fa3-0d0d-5167-94c2-8ea479a334e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371910} {:answer/id #uuid "222f9a03-a010-5d70-a31b-68c15b8ab921" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371911} {:answer/id #uuid "86cdb592-c0ee-5680-989f-fa4c9b6fb697" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9175a99b-56d0-595b-86db-511244542711" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371909} :answer-field/id #uuid "579f73d3-5ee3-5e3b-9cb0-45b6635a75cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371907}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f411b679-c6ae-56bb-9f50-ac0896593964" :question/math-academy-id "q-160885" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y+2x,\\,v=y-2x,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the lines $y+2x=1,$ $y-2x=1$ and the curve ${y}^{2}-4{x}^{2}=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=y+2x,\\,v=y-2x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $y+2x=u$ into the equation of the first line, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2x=1\\,⟹\\,u=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y-2x=v$ into the equation of the second line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2x=1\\,⟹\\,v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $u=y+2x$ and $v=y-2x$ into the equation of curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4{x}^{2}=4\\,⟹\\,(y+2x)(y-2x)=4,\\,⟹\\,uv=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection point of the two lines in the $uv$-plane is $(1,1).$\n\nTo find the intersection of the curve with the first line in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}uv &=4 \\\\ u &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=1 \\\\ v &=4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the intersections of the curve with the second line in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}uv &=4 \\\\ v &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=4 \\\\ v &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the intersection points are $(1,1),$ $(1,4)$ and $(4,1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,1\\le v\\le \\frac{4}{u}\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/30/30fff9564451baadff3cae48dbfb91dc08fc8591e596e7cd2f65d8cfc6c9c5a6.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(4)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{4}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{1}^{4/u}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}[v]_{1}^{4/u}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}(\\frac{4}{u}-1)du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[4\\operatorname{ln}⁡u-u]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-4+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-3) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373311 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e834b2d-26ec-5a0b-b024-653762ca7f8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373313} {:answer/id #uuid "8232f0ab-a3f1-5a06-a6cb-8418ed7a1099" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373314} {:answer/id #uuid "36c18b51-d2d9-5561-b308-2fdc610bb877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373315} {:answer/id #uuid "88c8bbb9-1718-53bf-8d76-f55389c1603f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373316} {:answer/id #uuid "29ff210b-32a3-59a3-9402-2b61a8d6a02a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8232f0ab-a3f1-5a06-a6cb-8418ed7a1099" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373314} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e5a6329-2104-5c1b-84f2-ccc6d5d0b453" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373312}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "db42860a-5849-59fe-9226-d7cef72a335f" :question/math-academy-id "q-82878" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v=y+1,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x-y)^{2}+y=2$ and the line $y=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v=y+1\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x-y=u$ and $y=v+1$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-y)^{2}+y=2\\,⟹\\,(y+1)=3-(x-y)^{2}\\,⟹\\,v=3-{u}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhile substituting $y=v-1$ into the equation of the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=0\\,⟹\\,v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=3-{u}^{2} \\\\ v &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=\\pm \\sqrt{2} \\\\ v &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(u,v)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}-\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2},\\hspace{1em}1\\le v\\le 3-{u}^{2}.\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/9c/9cec7ff29557aa14cd19309e9180482a44091e7ef215205285822674e7bc25b0.png)\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}[{\\int }_{1}^{3-{u}^{2}}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}[v]_{1}^{3-{u}^{2}}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(3-{u}^{2}-1)\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(2-{u}^{2})\\,du \\\\  & ={[2u-\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}]}_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =(2\\sqrt{2}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})-(-2\\sqrt{2}+\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373332 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e39adcf-f9fe-5dd8-8121-889b47f3e44f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373334} {:answer/id #uuid "eddeac90-01c0-5833-a667-85aceff8ed1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373335} {:answer/id #uuid "50017198-4384-5924-9339-b114ae17f28f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373336} {:answer/id #uuid "2b3d1083-137d-51cd-af77-24049d080386" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373337} {:answer/id #uuid "19278225-d301-581a-a354-aa480305af42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b3d1083-137d-51cd-af77-24049d080386" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373337} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0492151-0ea1-564c-95ed-c1870ce912b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373333}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "851a883c-83e2-57d3-b805-41682b65a2ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-83943" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x-2y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $y-(x-2y)^{2}=2$ and the line $y=3.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x-2y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x-2y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(x-2y)^{2}=2\\,⟹\\,v-{u}^{2}=2\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2}+2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=3\\,⟹\\,v=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &={u}^{2}+2 \\\\ v &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=\\pm 1 \\\\ v &=3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 1,3).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,{u}^{2}+2\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/ca/ca755cd11609e7c704adb37eb867ff8cc48bc5fc44b29cd777269be5791d9160.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|1|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{{u}^{2}+2}^{3}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[v]_{{u}^{2}+2}^{3}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3-({u}^{2}+2))\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-{u}^{2})\\,du \\\\  & ={[u-\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373339 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b76d7d05-c899-566f-a267-b260f4f22494" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373341} {:answer/id #uuid "e610ef2b-0037-56d0-bead-5ff13607271a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373342} {:answer/id #uuid "0ed3876d-8c80-5bc3-9185-9669f13f9f35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373343} {:answer/id #uuid "639ae9f3-3123-5bb0-aa7c-f883873eebe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373344} {:answer/id #uuid "8823d84a-4e7a-5110-b70c-63b3e1900363" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{47}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ed3876d-8c80-5bc3-9185-9669f13f9f35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373343} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3452f16-065c-5d3f-b342-bf97b568f3a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373340}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "672b45e6-0aa1-59c9-9e64-dd97d59af187" :question/math-academy-id "q-83947" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y+3x,\\,v=x,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(y+3x)^{2}-x=3$ and the line $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=y+3x,\\,v=x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $y+3x=u$ and $x=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3x)^{2}-x=3\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}-v=3\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2}-3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=1\\,⟹\\,v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &={u}^{2}-3 \\\\ v &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=\\pm 2 \\\\ v &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 2,1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,{u}^{2}-3\\le v\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/26/2687f0cc14eb0b476ea5f4ba7272e7f248bb7dcb276a0e2b0c39e551501b011d.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(-1)^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-1|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[{\\int }_{{u}^{2}-3}^{1}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[v]_{{u}^{2}-3}^{1}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(1-({u}^{2}-3))\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(4-{u}^{2})\\,du \\\\  & ={[4u-\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}]}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =16-\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411866 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ea71a81-55b2-5821-a85d-2c2b860ef761" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411729} {:answer/id #uuid "68da978e-5392-5c13-80d0-e709f72c6da6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411734} {:answer/id #uuid "6e0953bd-cd3e-5daf-9baa-978871a2e773" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411737} {:answer/id #uuid "98fb62ce-1156-5b60-96a5-36d7a22956c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411759} {:answer/id #uuid "a31dfc4c-cfac-5c12-8881-aa0d90cb5c66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68da978e-5392-5c13-80d0-e709f72c6da6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411734} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e959dbb-ec9c-5316-8bef-b8459b6fb499" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411827}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8fec53f8-0962-53b9-8a63-104c832ed68f" :question/math-academy-id "q-160710" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v=x+y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the lines $x+y=1,$ $x-y=1$ and the curve ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v=x+y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x+y=v$ into the equation of the first line, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y=1\\,⟹\\,v=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x-y=u$ into the equation of the second line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y=1\\,⟹\\,u=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x+y=v$ and $x-y=u$ into the equation of curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=2\\,⟹\\,(x-y)(x+y)=2,\\,⟹\\,uv=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection point of the two lines in the $uv$-plane is $(1,1).$\n\nTo find the intersection of the curve with the first line in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}uv &=2 \\\\ v &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=2 \\\\ v &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the intersections of the curve with the second line in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}uv &=2 \\\\ u &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=1 \\\\ v &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the intersection points are $(1,1),$ $(1,2)$ and $(2,1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le \\frac{2}{u}\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/4c/4c151e7fc8d150b9c8def69d609446b77424c2703decd9c8c55866cff2ae7437.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(2)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{2/u}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}[v]_{1}^{2/u}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{2}{u}-1)du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[2\\operatorname{ln}⁡u-u]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492015 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26ffbc26-848e-5d04-abcc-6404b93bc783" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491534} {:answer/id #uuid "51513ac6-c10d-5428-81d7-fc0f9ff5e5a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491608} {:answer/id #uuid "8d66c296-5953-5fbe-8ee0-08a430a0fe71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491694} {:answer/id #uuid "ab6be4c3-4f0a-5a3d-9787-10ec2aa91cac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491751} {:answer/id #uuid "df621dfa-555c-59a4-b00c-5eb1cda1d6b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26ffbc26-848e-5d04-abcc-6404b93bc783" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491534} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c1d39b3-e468-5801-801a-6497da6d4261" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491946}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "57f9fe20-2df7-5d01-bf45-354e1e66779e" :question/math-academy-id "q-38132" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+2y,\\,v=x,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x+2y)^{2}-x-1=0$ and the line $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+2y,\\,v=x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x+2y=u$ and $x=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2y)^{2}-x-1=0\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}-v-1=0\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2}-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=1\\,⟹\\,v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &={u}^{2}-1 \\\\ v &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=\\pm \\sqrt{2} \\\\ v &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,{u}^{2}-1\\le v\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/0c/0c598c1c0d09fb26175cad17106bc4529165da619f35a5882ead0ba133d05c2c.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}={(-2)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}[{\\int }_{{u}^{2}-1}^{1}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}[v]_{{u}^{2}-1}^{1}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(1-({u}^{2}-1))du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(2-{u}^{2})du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[2u-\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}]}_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((2\\sqrt{2}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})-(-2\\sqrt{2}+\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492060 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e9418b6-f97a-56c0-a5f8-cb663c2070de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491482} {:answer/id #uuid "1e609f4f-6be3-5c74-b9cf-1200ed558b19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491517} {:answer/id #uuid "234a9c24-6945-5264-98b6-b6c065eb22c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491527} {:answer/id #uuid "74cdbe9e-bfc8-55b1-addc-c01619e14194" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491661} {:answer/id #uuid "ba55a64c-3c7e-522a-800b-186dd45e7f5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e9418b6-f97a-56c0-a5f8-cb663c2070de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491482} :answer-field/id #uuid "462ba4b0-a9c1-5d72-894e-ca9fc93df274" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491908}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cfee0347-870f-5cb0-bd74-a4488b93243b" :question/math-academy-id "q-83944" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $y+(x-y)^{4}=3$ and the line $y=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x-y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+(x-y)^{4}=3\\,⟹\\,v+{u}^{4}=3\\,⟹\\,v=3-{u}^{4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2\\,⟹\\,v=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=3-{u}^{4} \\\\ v &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=\\pm 1 \\\\ v &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 1,2).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,2\\le v\\le 3-{u}^{4}\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/81/81ff220903cbe53cebf5e8f8b704e2a316be6092e1f14e527a7321e8f61bffb8.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|1|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{2}^{3-{u}^{4}}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[v]_{2}^{3-{u}^{4}}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}((3-{u}^{4})-2)\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-{u}^{4}))\\,du \\\\  & ={[u-\\frac{{u}^{5}}{5}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17418eda-e303-583e-abb8-8163f34cf65c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498779} {:answer/id #uuid "3b1f2bf6-1e84-5da8-8223-f7a8617abe63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498865} {:answer/id #uuid "aaa17f5a-3932-5a85-be4a-9e7f8aca50c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499103} {:answer/id #uuid "af9d5967-0e62-5e9c-80be-bb9563606efe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499116} {:answer/id #uuid "e507ffc4-50dc-56ba-8db0-2ce573a2305a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499248}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b1f2bf6-1e84-5da8-8223-f7a8617abe63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498865} :answer-field/id #uuid "06575685-8b6e-5313-b708-d919a2eb705b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499310}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5e32ec93-9a95-5b63-b1e8-8733a4d5c7af" :question/math-academy-id "q-82910" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curves $(x+y)^{3}-y=0$ and $(x+y)^{2}-y=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x+y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+y)^{3}-y=0\\,⟹\\,{u}^{3}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x+y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+y)^{2}-y=0\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &={u}^{3} \\\\ v &={u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}(u-1) &=0 \\\\ v &=u.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(0,0)$ and $(1,1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,{u}^{3}\\le v\\le {u}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/a8/a8d2549c665a918cc8d69c69136fcb49e654ebb9aa5e53611155798e791b36a9.png)\n\n   \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|1|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{u}^{3}}^{{u}^{2}}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}v{|}_{{u}^{3}}^{{u}^{2}}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}-{u}^{3})du \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{u}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558856 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ae34a70-04dd-51c4-956e-e0526d0f083b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{512}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558860} {:answer/id #uuid "b27db125-1811-506c-a9a7-73bd26f5a681" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{256}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558896} {:answer/id #uuid "c881d35c-8be6-578a-ac79-fe3e965182f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{128}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558905} {:answer/id #uuid "f09c7440-efea-5620-96c8-cd2af8b7e0e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{256}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558921} {:answer/id #uuid "f6645ca5-5ae8-52cc-a989-bcfcdd7ee0db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f09c7440-efea-5620-96c8-cd2af8b7e0e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{256}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558921} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2cc8718-1eda-50bf-b9d7-8164072450d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558910}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0ec47881-0e4e-5e03-9b0e-45477c45f447" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x - 6 y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x - 6 y)^{2}-(y)=0$ and the line $y=16.$" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - 6 y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - 6 y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - 6 y)^{2}-(y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=16.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=u^{2} \\\\ v &=16\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 4,16).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-4\\le u\\le 4,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 16\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/38/387e22e062c4ff7f24d26561fe369041f11f59a2b0305f8dc7b3ce49d411031a.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-6\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{16}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}[v]_{u^{2}}^{16}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}(16)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}(16 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 16 u]_{-4}^{4} \\\\ &=1(((64)+\\left(- \\frac{64}{3}\\right))-((-64)+\\left(\\frac{64}{3}\\right))) \\\\ &=1\\left(\\frac{256}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{256}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558865 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04e022d6-9b4b-55e1-b7b6-ebb206669d3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558852} {:answer/id #uuid "0d8e6678-6b99-5782-a45c-738ef21e7fe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558855} {:answer/id #uuid "24ba8bdc-8e30-56a6-ba09-574da792b447" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558864} {:answer/id #uuid "8b39f368-2d1d-5ed3-9ac5-7ba0648583de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558888} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c28887-3141-5343-a694-92ac663af400" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24ba8bdc-8e30-56a6-ba09-574da792b447" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558864} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bc4fd0a-f557-541f-8f97-4db32a398437" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558869}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "26c782a3-117c-52e4-894f-257331d9e2f3" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x - 8 y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x - 8 y)^{2}-(y)=0$ and the line $y=9.$" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - 8 y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - 8 y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - 8 y)^{2}-(y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=9.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=u^{2} \\\\ v &=9\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 3,9).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-3\\le u\\le 3,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 9\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/18/18c4c61a55dd2a361b82a208eb9a0d26f3fcfa213fe83b05f23644c9f79becaa.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-8\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{-3}^{3}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{9}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-3}^{3}[v]_{u^{2}}^{9}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-3}^{3}(9)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-3}^{3}(9 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 9 u]_{-3}^{3} \\\\ &=1(((27)+(-9))-((-27)+(9))) \\\\ &=1(36) \\\\ &=36\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558868 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27d774f4-7aa1-53ef-bfff-e5c57da8b9db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558866} {:answer/id #uuid "4bf81fa8-fdda-58ba-a730-3d93182c45ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "432" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558876} {:answer/id #uuid "d1f5de92-b1be-5ef3-8c2e-9dd32a242b99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "576" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558909} {:answer/id #uuid "df2bfe06-7bb3-5a53-b1bd-526ecc07d8b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558914} {:answer/id #uuid "f867445f-eeb9-5bb8-ac4e-ccb29ea58e2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27d774f4-7aa1-53ef-bfff-e5c57da8b9db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558866} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dd1ea61-ce15-535d-b216-c6fc2cd02f9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558878}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "29d96b47-2528-56e8-a582-384c42fdef27" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x - 4 y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x - 4 y)^{2}-(y)=0$ and the line $y=36.$" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - 4 y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - 4 y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - 4 y)^{2}-(y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=36.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=u^{2} \\\\ v &=36\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 6,36).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-6\\le u\\le 6,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 36\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/13/132cec4e3586ef176e2c94e05bbe8860063cce2cef4d0835159da844ea43b8ae.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-4\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{-6}^{6}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{36}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-6}^{6}[v]_{u^{2}}^{36}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-6}^{6}(36)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-6}^{6}(36 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 36 u]_{-6}^{6} \\\\ &=1(((216)+(-72))-((-216)+(72))) \\\\ &=1(288) \\\\ &=288\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558872 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5760816a-e7c5-5515-ab4a-83eb16e46ffe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{256}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558877} {:answer/id #uuid "8aebe7d3-4a30-5378-ae62-21d1ea1bccc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558887} {:answer/id #uuid "8baa2b35-f9c3-5f1a-afb6-3b95bc5d3503" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{256}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558889} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc885b9-d385-5924-9215-d1cc198f9347" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{128}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558890} {:answer/id #uuid "99302851-9856-5f4e-b25b-65e31ac4175b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{512}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558891}] :answer-field/correct {:ans