(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $8 x - 2 y=u$ and $x + y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(8 x - 2 y)^{2}-(x + y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x + y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=16.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=u^{2} \\\\ v &=16\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 4,16).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-4\\le u\\le 4,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 16\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/38/387e22e062c4ff7f24d26561fe369041f11f59a2b0305f8dc7b3ce49d411031a.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}8&-2\\\\1&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(10)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{1}{10}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}\\int_{-4}^{4}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{16}dv]du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}\\int_{-4}^{4}[v]_{u^{2}}^{16}du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}\\int_{-4}^{4}(16)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}\\int_{-4}^{4}(16 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 16 u]_{-4}^{4} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}(((64)+\\left(- \\frac{64}{3}\\right))-((-64)+\\left(\\frac{64}{3}\\right))) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}\\left(\\frac{256}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{128}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558904 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c128895-028a-5333-bd5a-8ee667b07a6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558854} {:answer/id #uuid "ca8ec194-90f6-583a-9a4b-6db556579326" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558906} {:answer/id #uuid "dc16d887-5db7-5718-ab28-a3f95ddc848b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{4}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558912} {:answer/id #uuid "debaf4ed-2222-5306-b87c-ad969bf33fa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558913} {:answer/id #uuid "e7dbec56-34b1-53a9-a22b-aba486b38599" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7dbec56-34b1-53a9-a22b-aba486b38599" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558920} :answer-field/id #uuid "dadd3d78-081c-5068-a70c-3f770eafd54e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558911}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c7168dbd-f88c-576f-98c9-c412c7458ef0" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=5 x - 2 y,\\,v=x + y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(5 x - 2 y)^{2}-(x + y)=0$ and the line $x + y=1.$" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=5 x - 2 y,\\,v=x + y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $5 x - 2 y=u$ and $x + y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(5 x - 2 y)^{2}-(x + y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x + y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=1.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=u^{2} \\\\ v &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 1,1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/39/39d20dd045c97cae079f3839b4db071bd6b3b9ff133299ed4ae4d9421a55fee8.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}5&-2\\\\1&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(7)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{7}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{1}{7}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}\\int_{-1}^{1}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{1}dv]du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}\\int_{-1}^{1}[v]_{u^{2}}^{1}du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}\\int_{-1}^{1}(1)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}\\int_{-1}^{1}(1 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + u]_{-1}^{1} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}(((1)+\\left(- \\frac{1}{3}\\right))-((-1)+\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right))) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{4}{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558917 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e60f5e8-67d9-5947-9c0b-02d3e2848b7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558874} {:answer/id #uuid "623d947d-bfd7-5e1b-be6c-685935878d3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558880} {:answer/id #uuid "838009ad-af94-5c1e-9dc6-233cad98d542" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558885} {:answer/id #uuid "d1051dd5-4fb6-5657-bf68-8d1d9e1a8de1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{18}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558908} {:answer/id #uuid "f7d2af79-5742-5740-a01d-6a65765b9184" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558924}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e60f5e8-67d9-5947-9c0b-02d3e2848b7c" :answer/ty